arm64: dts: mediatek: gemini: cap right-port USB to full speed

The right port's MUSB host babbles at high speed with a real device. A CDC-NCM
USB ethernet adapter enumerated, registered its interface, ran for 78 seconds
and then died, reproducibly:

    cdc_ncm 1-1:2.0 eth0: register 'cdc_ncm' at usb-musb-hdrc.3.auto-1
    cdc_ncm 1-1:2.0 enx6c6e072b2e37: renamed from eth0
    musb-hdrc musb-hdrc.3.auto: Babble
    usb 1-1: USB disconnect, device number 2

The node's own comment already offered the rollback -- "If HS regresses, drop
back to full-speed (known good)" -- so take it. 12 Mbit/s is ample for what
this port is for: ssh, tailscale and a terminal, not throughput.

Not using "mediatek,babble-keep-session" (issue #27), which would keep high
speed and ignore the babble: babble means the host saw traffic it could not
frame, so ignoring it preserves a link that is corrupting data. A slower link
that is correct is the better trade.

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
index 43e9f9d..a95878f 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6797-gemini-pda.dts
@@ -1927,8 +1927,23 @@
 		 * the num_eps=6 FIFO fix (usb/0002, build #254) removed the
 		 * actual defect, so high-speed (480 Mbps) is re-enabled.
 		 * If HS regresses, drop back to "full-speed" (known good).
+		 *
+		 * IT REGRESSED, 2026-08-27. A CDC-NCM USB ethernet adapter
+		 * enumerated and ran for 78 seconds, then:
+		 *     musb-hdrc musb-hdrc.3.auto: Babble
+		 *     usb 1-1: USB disconnect, device number 2
+		 * every time. So this takes the rollback the comment above
+		 * offers. Full speed is 12 Mbit/s, which is ample for ssh,
+		 * tailscale and a terminal -- the point of this port is a way
+		 * onto the network, not throughput.
+		 *
+		 * The alternative is "mediatek,babble-keep-session" (issue #27,
+		 * docs/usb11-hs-bulk-divergence.md), which ignores babble and
+		 * keeps the session up. Not chosen: babble means the host saw
+		 * traffic it could not frame, so ignoring it keeps a link that
+		 * is corrupting data. Prefer the slower link that is correct.
 		 */
-		maximum-speed = "high-speed";
+		maximum-speed = "full-speed";
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 };
